-- title:
--   Degree-zero principal divisors for separable extensions of K(t)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $E$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure and a $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$-algebra structure which are compatible, in the sense that $K \to \mathrm{RatFunc}\,K \to E$ is a scalar tower; assume $E$ is finite-dimensional over the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$ and that the extension $E/\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$ is separable. The conclusion is the class `HasPrincipalDivisors K E`, that is: for every $f \in E$ with $f \neq 0$ there exists a divisor $D$, meaning a finitely supported function from the places of $E$ over $K$ to $\mathbb{Z}$, such that $D\,v = v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ for every place $v$, and such that $D$ has degree $0$, the degree being $\sum_{v \in \operatorname{supp} D} D\,v \cdot \deg v$. Here a place of $E$ over $K$ is a valuation subring of $E$ which contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is not the whole of $E$, and is a principal ideal ring. Since $D$ is required to be finitely supported and to agree with $v \mapsto v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ at every place, the assertion includes the statement that $f$ has nonzero order at only finitely many places.
--
--   This is the degree-zero property of principal divisors for a global function field presented as a finite separable extension of $K(t)$, the basic ingredient in the divisor-class theory of curves over $K$. It is the form of the result used to deduce the same statement for an arbitrary separable extension generated by a transcendental element, in [`AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_transcendental_of_isSeparable`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_transcendental_of_isSeparable); the proof reduces to the rational function field case together with the conservation of degrees in the fibre of a place, via the sum of ramification indices times inertia degrees.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_hasPrincipalDivisors_of_finiteDimensional_of_isSeparable.lean

